.TH EUCLID-CLI-EES-RECEIVE-EVENTS 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ees-receive-events \- claim a subscriber's waiting events
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ees receive-events
.BR \-\-name | \-n
.I NAME
[\fB\-\-max\-events\fR|\fB\-m\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-wait\-time\fR|\fB\-w\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-\-visibility\-timeout\fR|\fB\-V\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-\-acknowledge\fR|\fB\-a\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Claims up to
.I N
of the events waiting for
.IR NAME ,
oldest first.
.PP
A claim is a lease, not a delete. The events it returns are invisible to
every other claimer until the visibility timeout runs out, and come back
if they are never acknowledged \- which is what makes a consumer that
crashes halfway through its work harmless: the event it was holding is
handed to the next claimer instead of being lost with it. Acknowledge
with
.BR euclid-cli-ees-ack-events (1)
once the work is actually done, or pass
.B \-\-acknowledge
here for the cases where reading the events is the work.
.PP
Each returned event carries its
.IR eventId ,
its type, the module that published it, its
.I payload
and
.IR attempts ,
which is how many times it has been claimed \- a value above one means an
earlier claimer never acknowledged it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Subscriber name to claim events for. Required.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-max\-events " " \fIN\fR
Largest number of events to claim. Defaults to 10.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-wait\-time " " \fISECONDS\fR
Seconds to wait for an event before answering with none. Defaults to 0.
Bounded at 20 by the server, because a waiting call holds a gateway
worker thread for its duration.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-visibility\-timeout " " \fISECONDS\fR
How long the claim holds before the events become claimable again.
Defaults to 300, deliberately more generous than the 30 seconds the bus
gives its own modules: a consumer here may be doing real work with an
event, such as downloading the object it describes, and having it
redelivered underneath itself is worse than waiting.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-acknowledge
Acknowledge the received events immediately, deleting them. The events
are printed before the acknowledgement is sent, so they are on screen
even if that call fails.
.SH NOTES
Without
.BR \-\-acknowledge ,
events received at a terminal simply reappear once the visibility
timeout runs out, so an unacknowledged look at a backlog costs nothing
but a delay.
.PP
Claiming under an application's subscriber name takes those events
.I from
it, since instances sharing a name are meant to share the work. To watch
a stream alongside a running application, subscribe under a name of your
own: both subscriptions then get their own copy of every event.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The call succeeded. The claimed events are printed as JSON, with
.I total
being how many there were \- zero is a normal answer.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, no session, the server rejected the
request, or \- with
.B \-\-acknowledge
\- the events were received but could not be acknowledged, in which case
they will be delivered again after the visibility timeout.
.SH EXAMPLES
Long-poll for up to twenty seconds, then acknowledge whatever came:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ees receive-events \e
    --name invoice-import --wait-time 20 --acknowledge
.fi
.RE
.PP
Look at what is waiting without taking it out of circulation for long:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ees receive-events \e
    --name invoice-import --max-events 5 --visibility-timeout 5
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees-ack-events (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees-subscribe-events (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees-list-subscriptions (1)
